        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.048,
            0.419,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.089,
            0.847,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "2. The average footfall in this theater before COVID was 160. Now it is just 100. The decrease in the footfall is 60."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.288,
            0.132,
            0.711,
            0.192
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r l } & { \\quad { P e r c e n t a g e \\quad d e c r e a s e = } \\quad \\frac { a m o u n t \\quad o f d e c r e a s e } { o r i g i n a l \\quad a m o u n t \\quad o r \\quad b a s e } \\times 1 0 0 } \\\\ & { \\quad = \\frac { 6 0 } { 1 6 0 } \\times 1 0 0 = 3 7 . 5 \\% . } \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.194,
            0.829,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "We say the footfall in this theater post-COVID has decreased by 37.5%."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.225,
            0.805,
            0.247
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Example 3: Do the following two statements mean the same thing?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.254,
            0.784,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": "(i) The population of this state in 1991 is 165% of that in 1961."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.278,
            0.847,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "(ii) The population of this state has increased by 65% from 1961 to 1991. Yes, both mean the same. Suppose p is the population of the state in 1961 and q is the population of the state in 1991."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.264,
            0.345,
            0.478,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "{ \\begin{array} { r l } & { { \\mathrm { S t a t e m e n t ~ A ~ i m p l i e s , } } } \\\\ & { \\quad q = 1 6 5 \\% { \\mathrm { ~ o f ~ } } p } \\\\ & { \\quad q = { \\frac { 1 6 5 } { 1 0 0 } } \\times p = 1 . 6 5 p } \\end{array} }",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.534,
            0.345,
            0.756,
            0.407
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}\\text { Statement B implies, } \\\\ {q=p+65 \\% \\text { of } p} \\\\ {q=p+0.65 \\times p=1.65 p}\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.419,
            0.846,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": "In other words, the population of the state in 1991 is 1.65 times that in 1961."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.469,
            0.317,
            0.488
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Profit and Loss"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.492,
            0.847,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "You may have the experience of buying something — snacks, groceries, clothes, toys, etc. Very often, the shopkeeper quotes a price and after some bargaining the customer pays the negotiated amount and buys the item(s). We call the price quoted by the shopkeeper the **marked price** (sometimes this can be the MRP of an item). The price the customer pays after a discount is called the **selling price**. Also, the price the shopkeeper paid to purchase that item is called the **cost price**. Let us see how these labels are relative to the context through an example."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.639,
            0.847,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": "The picture below shows the journey of a sweater from the manufacturer to the customer and what the cost price (CP), the marked price (MP), and the selling price (SP) mean in each step."
        },
